# a format with no conversion and arguments: the results are unspecified (item 9); macOS writes a diagnostic, we ignore them
'hello\n' a b
'100%%\n' a b
# a field width or precision too big for an int: we say so and stop, macOS does something else (unspecified)
'%99999999999d\n' 1
'%.99999999999s\n' 1
# what follows a backslash that is no escape sequence is unspecified: macOS drops the backslash, we write both
'\q|'
'%b\n' 'a\qb'
# \0 in %b is a byte with the value 0 (item 7); macOS ends the string there as a C string
'%b|\n' '\0|\01|\012|\0101|\01012'
# a quote and no character after it is not a number: we say so, macOS writes 0 without a message
"%d\n" "'"
# macOS reports the underflow of 1e-999 as an error; the standard names overflow (APPLICATION USAGE), and a number too small to hold is not an error here
'%f|%e|\n' 1e-999 1e-999
